{
  "note": [
    "The shared map-fanout transition table. One artefact, two runners for the",
    "provider-level cases, so the Rust map engine and the TypeScript worker over",
    "it cannot drift apart silently.",
    "",
    "`transitions` are single-step engine cases: descriptor state plus one",
    "event in, an ordered effect list or a rejection out. The map engine lives",
    "in Rust, so their runner is tests/map_transitions.rs, which drives them",
    "through `durust::map_engine::step`, a `#[doc(hidden)]` module exposed for",
    "that runner, and projects every effect onto the fields the table names.",
    "",
    "`fanouts` are scripted whole-fanout cases expressed only in terms of the",
    "public provider API \u2014 claim an item, complete it, fail it, read the",
    "parent's history. Both runtimes execute every one of them: the Rust runner",
    "over its providers, and packages/core/test/map-fanouts.test.ts over the Rust",
    "memory provider through @durust/native under the TypeScript contract."
  ],
  "exclusions": [
    {
      "what": "ScheduleItemRetry.visibleAtMs and .timeoutAtMs values",
      "why": "Both runtimes carry the same retry-policy fields (initialIntervalMs, maxIntervalMs, backoffCoefficient, maxAttempts) and Rust's `retry_delay_ms` computes the same delay, but the table asserts the shape only: `visibleAtMs === null` means immediately claimable, a timed-out attempt always yields null, and cases carry `visibleAtMs` as \"Null\" or \"Deferred\" and `timeoutAtMs` as \"Null\" or \"Present\" rather than as numbers. The delays themselves are pinned by the provider conformance scenarios (`memory_timeout_retry_becomes_visible_after_the_policy_backoff` and the map-item pacing scenarios) rather than by this table."
    },
    {
      "what": "The ParentCancelled event",
      "why": "Both runtimes now drive it from WorkflowTaskCommit::cancel_commands / cancelCommands, and both emit the same two effects, so the exclusion is no longer about reachability. It stays because the transition is only half the behaviour: cancelling a child map must also cancel the children it already started, and that is deliberately not a step effect \u2014 the other producer, a run reaching a terminal event, must leave its children to the parentClosePolicy path, which is free to abandon them. Each provider therefore pairs ParentCancelled with its own child cancellation, and a table that asserted only the effect list would pin the half that agrees and say nothing about the half that matters. Each engine's own unit tests cover the transition."
    }
  ],
